a memorable last scene can make a good  movie great but which marvel movie  really broke the mold what exactly was  nick cage doing in the wicker man how  did one british comedy inspire a  thousand funerals keep watching to find  out warning spoilers ahead for all the  movies discussed  fight club david fincher's 1999  adaptation of the 1996 chuck palahniuk  novel is one of those movies where the  big twist ending forces you to reassess  everything you've just seen  the reveal that tyler durden is the  narrator's alternate personality might  raise a little confusion about specific  plot mechanics but it immediately brings  the story's themes into focus tyler is  the unhealthy projection of the  narrator's own rage self-loathing and  toxic masculinity an internal antagonist  who needs to be defeated the climactic  twist comes straight from the book but  the movie offers its own spin on the  ultimate resolution though the narrator  kills tyler project mayhem actually  succeeds and tyler's carefully planted  bombs are detonated in the darkly  hilarious finale the narrator tells  marla you met me at a very strange time  in my life  buildings explode around them as the  pixies where is my mind leads into the  credits this ending was actually  censored in china although ironically  the censored version is closer to  palink's novel  in many ways dr strange is one of the  more formulaic marvel origin stories  with steven strange's character arc  having a lot in common with that of tony  stark in the first iron man movie what a  relief it is then when the climax of the  movie breaks significantly from the  standard mcu formula whereas most marvel  movies build up to giant fight scenes  between the heroes and villains scott  derrickson's 2016 film finds a more  clever method for the sorcerer supreme  to save earth from the cosmic terror of  durmamu strange's strategy initially  appears to be an attempted diplomacy he  announces his intention to bargain with  dormammu at which point the dark  dimension ruler promptly kills him it  soon becomes clear however that  strange's true plan is to annoy dormammu  into submission as he has trapped both  himself and or mamu in a time loop in  which strange keeps coming back to be  killed again and again and again  i've come to bargain  eventually dormammu gets so sick of the  loop that he agrees to strange's terms  to leave earth alone no doubt more  superhero movies could benefit from the  inventive use of superpowers displayed  in this unique showdown  director adam mckay's don't look up was  one of the most divisive films of 2021  it was one of netflix's most watched  movies ever and accrued many award  nominations including one for best  picture at the oscars but reviews were  mixed even those who generally disliked  the apocalyptic satire however have  acknowledged that the film is at its  best when it finds genuine pathos in the  character's final night on earth with  the ultra rich escaping to outer space  and the comet about to deliver the  extinction of mankind dr randall mindy  decides to spend one last dinner  together with friends and family ewell  the christian anarchist skater played by  timothy chalamet leads the group in  prayer before dinner and everyone  expresses the things they're grateful  for even as the world is literally  ending around them critics have  disagreed about the movie as a whole but  there's no denying the power of its  final moments  many of pixar's movies save their most  emotional moments for the end the ending  of monsters university the 2013 prequel  to monsters inc stands out by elevating  what is otherwise a cute but  unexceptional parody of college movies  into something that genuinely lives up  to pixar's high standard of quality  there are many family movies about  following your dreams but monsters  university is one of the few to  acknowledge that dreams can fail and  change no matter how hard he tries mike  wazowski realizes he's never going to be  scary that's okay though because he  finds there are other things he's good  at such as working as a scare assistant  mike  you're not scary  not even little but you are fearless  both mike and sully end up expelled for  all the trouble they cause but they find  they don't need to follow the set path  of academia to succeed instead they  apply for jobs in the mail room at  monsters inc and the credits sequence  shows them gradually working their way  up the corporate ladder it's an unusual  but positive message of flexibility and  compromise  writer director celine skiama is  talented at many things but her greatest  talent might be found in the endings to  her movies portrait of a lady on fire  her 2019 historical lesbian romance is a  slow burn with a powerful conclusion  romantic tensions gradually build up  between the painter marianne and her  aristocratic subject eloise but their  affair is brief and marianne only sees  eloise twice after it's over the first  time is at an art exhibit the second  time is at the orchestra and it's the  ending which turns the film into one of  the all-time great tearjerkers portrait  of a lady on fire uses music extremely  sparingly only in scenes where the  characters are actually performing or  singing as a result when music is heard  in the film its effect is extremely  powerful the orchestra in the final  scene is playing the presto from summer  in vivaldi's four seasons the same piece  of music marianne once played for eloise  eloise doesn't know marianne is  observing her in the audience for this  concert but when she hears this music  she appears overwhelmed with the  memories of her love it's an incredible  piece of filmmaking on skiama's part  supported by a superb performance by  adele hendell  at the start of 1973's f for fake  filmmaker orson welles assures the  audience  during the next hour everything you'll  hear from us is really true and based on  solid facts  he spends the next hour talking about  various real-life hoaxes from art  forgeries to the fictional news  broadcasts in his own 1938 war of the  worlds radio drama but f for fake runs  longer than an hour and in its final 28  minutes it becomes one of the very fakes  the first hour was documenting wells is  honest about his bakery of course all  his assurances of truth at the beginning  were clearly stated to last just one  hour and at the end of the film he  openly admits to lying his head off  during the 17 minute story about his  partner and co-writer oya kodar modeling  for and swindling picasso in an era of  fake news well's trolling deconstruction  of the documentary format is more  relevant than ever  christopher nolan's 2006 film the  prestige concludes with several  mind-blowing twists each of the  magicians is hiding secrets for hugh  jackman's robert angier the secret is  that he's able to pull off the  transported man magic trick through  actual magic of sorts cloning himself  with a machine built by nikola tesla  christian bale's alfred borden performs  the trick using a very different secret  he's not actually one person but twin  brothers sharing a single identity one  borden is hanged after being framed for  killing anger who's secretly still alive  under the identity of lord caldlow the  other borden gets his revenge and kills  the last remaining anger whose theater  burns down with all his dead clones  inside great twist endings are hard to  pull off but the prestige is a movie  designed around its many revelations  meticulously following the steps that  make a great magic trick work  set in a motel outside of disney world  the florida project centers around  mooney a six-year-old girl having fun  with her friends while her loving but  neglectful single mother haley struggles  to make ends meet it's a movie that's  repetitive by design to best get across  the reality of life in poverty but the  ending shakes up this repetition in a  manner both heartbreaking and absolutely  stunning  eventually things get bad enough that  child services come to take mooney away  from haley in this moment of deep pain  mooney and her friend janzi escape and  run away to the magic kingdom people  have debated whether the disney world  scene is meant to be real or the  children's fantasy but however you  interpret it the conclusion makes the  film as a whole even more memorable and  affecting  fans might argue over which monty python  movie is the funniest but monty python's  life of brian is definitely the one with  the strongest story one which leads to  the strongest ending by far this  irreverent often censored 1979 comedy  follows brian a very naughty boy living  in proximity to jesus who is  accidentally mistaken for the messiah at  the end of the film brian has been  sentenced to death by crucifixion and  all attempts to save him go horribly  awry reg what are you going to do come  on  and thanks  unable to do anything to escape his slow  and painful death the other crucified  men cheer brian up by singing always  look on the bright side of life  optimistic nihilistic absurdist  extremely british there are lots of ways  you can describe the comedic  juxtaposition of eric idol's silly  upbeat song with the direness of its  context whatever you want to call it  though it clearly struck a chord in 2014  always look on the bright side of life  was revealed to have been the most  frequently requested funeral song in the  united kingdom  neil labute's 2006 remake of the wicker  man is not considered a good horror  movie by most people's standards in the  final moments of its unrated cut however  it certainly becomes memorable though  there are several amusing displays of  overacting from nicolas cage throughout  the film none compares to the scene  where the villagers torture him with  these resulting in the now legendary  line love the beast  i love my eyes  in a 2022 interview with indy wire cage  claimed the comedy of this scene was  intentional and that he and labute  actually wanted the film to go further  with the ridiculousness he said it  probably would have been more clear how  funny it was if producer abby lerner let  me have the handlebar mustache that i  wanted to wear and be burned in the bear  suit that would have been so horrifying  but they didn't go for that because all  the comedy would have emerged from this  horror he pointed to the horrifying yet  also darkly comedic conclusion of  midsummer as a more successful example  of what the wickerman remake was aiming  for michael curtis's 1942 drama  casablanca is a movie that's so beloved  as a classic that basically every line  has become a cliche and it's still a  delight to watch 80 years later from  beginning to end casablanca is a  brilliantly entertaining movie with  clever writing and captivating romantic  chemistry between humphrey bogart's rick  blaine and ingrid bergman's ilsa lund  it's the story's conclusion however that  truly seals this as one of the greatest  american films ever made rick a  character who was once a celebrated  freedom fighter but has since become  cynical and disillusioned regains his  heroism at a time when the world truly  needs it he sacrifices his change to  stay with ilsa in order to help her and  her husband escape from the nazis he's  looking at you kid  indeed casablanca is a work of wartime  propaganda made with such moral heft and  great storytelling that it elevates  itself to become a powerful work of art  george romero's knight of the living  dead shocked moviegoers in 1968 with its  groundbreaking gore and fresh take on  the zombie mythos for all the terror of  its flesh-eating undead though the  ending of the film shows how ordinary  humans can be the scariest monsters of  all ben the movie's black hero has done  his best to survive throughout the film  but in the end he's killed by some white  men with guns who seemingly mistake him  for a zombie romero didn't intentionally  write knight of the living dead with  race in mind but regardless of what the  filmmaker initially intended the ending  was widely viewed as a subversive  commentary on racism its implications  are unfortunately no less scary today  than they were when the movie was first  released you can credit night of the  living dead and particularly its ending  for helping lead the way for such  racially conscious horror films as  candyman and get out check out one of  our newest videos right here plus even  more looper videos about your favorite  movies are coming soon subscribe to our  youtube channel and hit the bell so you  don't miss a single one  [Applause]  [Music]
 

